    "Social identity theory proposes that people define part of their self-concept by the groups they belong to, and that they boost self-esteem by viewing their own group (the in-group) more favorably than other groups (out-groups). In the classic \"minimal group\" experiments, people were assigned to groups on trivial grounds yet still favored their own group.",
    "In a hypothetical study, 160 nursing students were told a short test showed they either \"overestimated\" or \"underestimated\" the number of dots in pictures; in fact, group labels were assigned at random. Participants never met other group members and gained nothing personally from their choices. Each participant then divided 16 reward points between two anonymous students, one from each group.",
